 "169df782-f22f-53ee-888a-ef5dd9042bbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559096 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559096 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559096 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2155da94-081e-506a-911b-03855a5f2cbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559524 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559524 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559524 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d0e04b3f-f522-5411-be37-966761ea2f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559557 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559557 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559557 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dbcd5536-0adc-5f39-92ab-7c7a8048c753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559582 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8356a21-b88d-5892-a5fb-1e440a90d6f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559764 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4be26dd-2c06-59f0-a37a-9169047bccde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda -7)^{3}(\\lambda +4)^{2}(\\lambda -9)^{9},$ the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ and the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e1e17bba-2392-58cc-bb67-1a8adce57d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559872 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $D$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(D-9I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(D-9I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{2}$ equals $11,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=14-11=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ of dimension $3$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(D-9I)^{3}$ equals $8,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}=14-8=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(D-9I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ of dimension $6$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $3$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(D-9I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(D-9I)^{2}=6-3=3\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
